data.content:
Here are the recommended MSTest replacements:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetActiveUsers_ReturnsOnlyActiveUsers()
{
    var service = new UserService();
    var users = service.GetActiveUsers();

    // Assert.IsTrue(users.Count > 0)
    Assert.IsTrue(users.Count > 0); // or CollectionAssert.That.IsNotEmpty() if using MSTest v3+

    // Assert.IsTrue(users.All(u => u.IsActive))
    CollectionAssert.AllItemsAreNotNull(users);
    // For predicate checks, use:
    Assert.IsTrue(users.All(u => u.IsActive)); // no built-in equivalent

    // Assert.IsTrue(users.Count() == 3)
    Assert.AreEqual(3, users.Count);

    // Assert.IsTrue(admin != null)
    var admin = users.Single(u => u.Role == "Admin");
    Assert.IsNotNull(admin);
}
```

**Key replacements:**

| Instead of | Use |
|---|---|
| `Assert.IsTrue(x.Count > 0)` | `CollectionAssert.That is not empty` — or just `Assert.AreNotEqual(0, users.Count)` |
| `Assert.IsTrue(col.Count == n)` | `Assert.AreEqual(n, col.Count)` |
| `Assert.IsTrue(obj != null)` | `Assert.IsNotNull(obj)` |
| checking all items match | `CollectionAssert.AllItemsAreUnique()`, `.Contains()`, `.AreEquivalent()` |

**Other useful `CollectionAssert` methods:**
- `CollectionAssert.Contains(collection, element)`
- `CollectionAssert.DoesNotContain(collection, element)`
- `CollectionAssert.AreEquivalent(expected, actual)` — same items, any order
- `CollectionAssert.AreEqual(expected, actual)` — same items, same order
- `CollectionAssert.IsSubsetOf(subset, superset)`

MSTest doesn't have a built-in "all items match predicate" assertion, so `Assert.IsTrue(users.All(...))` is acceptable there—or consider adding a helper method for better failure messages.
